<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Andrea Mantegna</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1431–<a href="/years/1506">1506</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Andrea Mantegna (c. 1431–1506) was an Italian painter of the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>, trained in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Padua</a>. From 1460 he served as court painter to the Gonzaga in Mantua, where he decorated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camera-degli-sposi">Camera degli Sposi</a> in the ducal castle. His work is known for its sculptural figures, bold perspective and close study of Roman antiquity.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/andrea-mantegna.jpg" alt="Andrea Mantegna" width="320" height="424" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gian Marco Cavalli [?] · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mantegna_Bronze_Bust_San_Andrea_Mantua.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Mantegna was born at Isola di Carturo, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Padua</a>, in the Venetian Republic, the second son of a carpenter named Biagio. At 11 he was apprenticed to the Paduan painter Francesco Squarcione, who collected antique statues, reliefs and vases for others to study, and who taught him <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> and set him to study fragments of Roman sculpture. At 17 Mantegna left the workshop, later claiming that Squarcione had profited from his work without paying him enough.</p><p>In 1448 he was called to the team painting the Ovetari Chapel in the church of the Eremitani and finished most of it alone. Almost all of the cycle was lost in the Allied bombing of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Padua</a> in 1944. In 1453 Jacopo Bellini consented to the marriage of his daughter Nicolosia to Mantegna.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Mantegna left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Padua</a> young and never returned. Between 1457 and 1459 he painted the San Zeno Altarpiece in Verona. In 1460 Marquis Ludovico III Gonzaga appointed him court artist at Mantua, on a salary of 75 lire a month, and from December 1466 he lived there. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camera-degli-sposi">Camera degli Sposi</a> in the ducal palace, presumably finished in 1474, has portraits of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-gonzaga">Gonzaga family</a>, and its spatial construction, especially the ceiling oculus, greatly influenced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-da-correggio">Correggio</a>.</p><p>In 1488 Pope Innocent VIII called him to paint frescoes in a Vatican chapel, later destroyed under Pius VI in 1780. Back in Mantua in 1490, he worked for Isabella d'Este and finished around 1492 the nine tempera Triumphs of Caesar, sold in 1628 to King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-i-of-england">Charles I of England</a>. He and his workshop also led the production of prints in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> before 1500.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Mantegna died at Mantua on 13 September 1506, in financial difficulty and forced to sell an antique bust of Faustina. His sons set up a monument to him in the church of Sant'Andrea in 1516. His figures are sculptural and his landscapes stony, and his main legacy is considered to be spatial illusionism, a tradition of ceiling decoration followed for almost three centuries. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giovanni-bellini">Giovanni Bellini</a> followed him in his early works, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albrecht-durer">Albrecht Dürer</a> reproduced several of his engravings, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-da-correggio">Correggio</a> built on his perspective research in the dome of Parma Cathedral.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Andrea Mantegna” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Andrea Mantegna</b> painted the Camera degli Sposi for Ludovico III; Isabella d'Este collected art; Giulio Romano built the Palazzo Te for Federico II.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-mantua">Duchy of Mantua</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Isola Mantegna, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in Mantua. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.57,11.73&amp;year=1479" aria-label="Isola Mantegna on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4828 557 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Andrea Mantegna"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5278 827h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5278 827h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.57,11.73&amp;year=1479">What was here in 1479? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
